{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-19034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $a\\cdot b=-8$, find the angle between $a=⟨2t,2t⟩$ and $b=⟨4,-2⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-19034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, since $a\\cdot b=-8$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =-8 \\\\ 2t\\cdot 4+2t\\cdot (-2) & =-8 \\\\ 8t-4t & =-8 \\\\ 4t & =-8 \\\\ t & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨2(-2),\\,2(-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $a$ and $b$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})≈{108.4}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-19034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78041014569dbb2eb920179920355104fa61b75704067f0685f412217be6959c"} {"question" "q-19034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-19034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{106.8}^{∘}"] ["math" "{108.4}^{∘}"] ["math" "{117.1}^{∘}"] ["math" "{86.9}^{∘}"] ["math" "{99.3}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-19034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{108.4}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-69833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $∠XZY$ given the points $X(-1,0,3)$, $Y(-2,2,3)$, and $Z(-2,-1,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-69833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $X$, $Y$, $Z$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=⟨-1,0,3⟩,\\,y=⟨-2,2,3⟩,\\,z=⟨-2,-1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△XYZ$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠XZY=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/assets/87453fecb9da6b9e673ab5a85a289a3fac73f4fb90fc8f1b33e7c0a80b1ea77f.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ZX} & =x-z \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,3⟩-⟨-2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{ZY} & =y-z \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,3⟩-⟨-2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{ZX}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{ZY}}{|\\overset{\\to }{ZX}||\\overset{\\to }{ZY}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-69833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78041014569dbb2eb920179920355104fa61b75704067f0685f412217be6959c"} {"question" "q-69833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-69833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{120}^{∘}"] ["math" "{30}^{∘}"] ["math" "{45}^{∘}"] ["math" "{60}^{∘}"] ["math" "{90}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-69833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{45}^{∘}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-101507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(3,3,-5)$, $Q(4,2,-3)$, and $R(7,5,-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-101507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨3,3,-5⟩,\\,q=⟨4,2,-3⟩,\\,r=⟨7,5,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨4,2,-3⟩-⟨3,3,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨7,5,-3⟩-⟨3,3,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{24}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-101507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78041014569dbb2eb920179920355104fa61b75704067f0685f412217be6959c"} {"question" "q-101507" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-101507" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{180}^{∘}"] ["math" "{30}^{∘}"] ["math" "{45}^{∘}"] ["math" "{60}^{∘}"] ["math" "{90}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-101507" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{60}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-101509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(-5,1,3)$, $Q(-3,-1,2)$, and $R(-5,2,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-101509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨-5,1,3⟩,\\,q=⟨-3,-1,2⟩,\\,r=⟨-5,2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1,2⟩-⟨-5,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨-5,2,4⟩-⟨-5,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={135}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-101509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78041014569dbb2eb920179920355104fa61b75704067f0685f412217be6959c"} {"question" "q-101509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-101509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{120}^{∘}"] ["math" "{135}^{∘}"] ["math" "{150}^{∘}"] ["math" "{180}^{∘}"] ["math" "{90}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-101509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{135}^{∘}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-125078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $a\\cdot b=-9$, find the angle between $a=⟨-2t,3t⟩$ and $b=⟨3,1⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-125078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, since $a\\cdot b=-9$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =-9 \\\\ -2t\\cdot 3+3t\\cdot 1 & =-9 \\\\ -6t+3t & =-9 \\\\ -3t & =-9 \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨-2(3),\\,3(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $a$ and $b$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9}{\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{9}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{117}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{3\\sqrt{13}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{130}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{130}})≈{105.3}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-125078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78041014569dbb2eb920179920355104fa61b75704067f0685f412217be6959c"} {"question" "q-125078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-125078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{100.5}^{∘}"] ["math" "{105.3}^{∘}"] ["math" "{115.2}^{∘}"] ["math" "{123.2}^{∘}"] ["math" "{95.9}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"} {"question" "q-125078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{105.3}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/state.json" "file_sha256" "5758995502251e198d32ba21d5bc1d0937f7a6aed2695c219b5fc2a158ae360f"}] "usage" {"q-19034" []} "basis" 1317 "retractions" [[17592186106030 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $a \\cdot b =-8$, find the angle between $a = ⟨2t, 2t⟩$ and $b = ⟨4,-2⟩$. Round you